================================================================================
  BACKEND — DETAILED FILE DESCRIPTIONS
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  backend/main.py
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The core of the backend. Initialises the FastAPI application and wires together
all components: CORS, RAG pipeline, LLM, and ML model.

  App Setup:
    - Creates FastAPI instance titled "Insurance AI Platform"
    - Adds CORSMiddleware allowing all methods/headers from http://localhost:5173
      (the Vite dev server) so the frontend can call the API without CORS errors

  RAG Setup (runs at startup):
    - Loads HuggingFaceEmbeddings using model "sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"
      This model runs locally — no external API call for embeddings
    - Loads the FAISS vector store from "insurance_faiss_db/" using those embeddings
      allow_dangerous_deserialization=True is set because the index was built locally
    - Instantiates ChatMistralAI with model "open-mistral-7b", temperature=0.1
      API key is read from the MISTRAL_API_KEY environment variable via .env

  ML Model Setup (runs at startup):
    - Loads insurance_premium_model.pkl using joblib.load()

  clean_response(text) helper:
    - Strips markdown bold (**text**) and italic (__text__) markers
    - Removes heading markers (# ## ###)
    - Removes horizontal rule lines (---)
    - Removes table pipe formatting
    - Collapses excessive blank lines
    - Returns clean plain text suitable for the chat UI

  API Routes:

    GET /
      Returns: { "status": "Insurance RAG + Premium Prediction API Running" }
      Purpose: Health check to confirm the server is up

    POST /chat
      Request body: { "question": "string" }
      Steps:
        1. Calls db.similarity_search(question, k=3) to find the 3 most
           semantically similar document chunks from the FAISS index
        2. Joins their page_content into a context string
        3. Builds a prompt instructing the LLM to act as an Indian health
           insurance expert, use short paragraphs, avoid markdown, be concise
        4. Calls llm.invoke(prompt) — sends to Mistral AI cloud
        5. Runs clean_response() on the result
        6. Returns: { "answer": "cleaned plain text response" }

    POST /predict
      Request body: { age, sex, bmi, children, smoker, disease, policy_type }
      Steps:
        1. Wraps the request fields in a pandas DataFrame (single row)
        2. Calls model.predict(df) — runs XGBoost inference
        3. Returns: { "premium": integer value in INR }

  Pydantic Models:
    ChatRequest   — validates { question: str }
    PredictRequest — validates { age: int, sex: int, bmi: float,
                                  children: int, smoker: int,
                                  disease: int, policy_type: int }

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  backend/vector_store.py
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A standalone one-time setup script. Run this ONCE before starting the server
to build and save the FAISS vector database from the JSON knowledge base.

  Steps:
    1. Calls load_documents() to get all LangChain Document objects
    2. Splits them using RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter:
         chunk_size=400 characters, chunk_overlap=50 characters
       This ensures long documents are broken into manageable pieces while
       preserving context at chunk boundaries
    3. Creates HuggingFaceEmbeddings (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) — runs locally
    4. Builds a FAISS vector store from all chunks + embeddings
    5. Saves it to "insurance_faiss_db/" (creates index.faiss + index.pkl)
    6. Prints total chunk count and a success message

  Do NOT run this while the server is running. Run it once during setup.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  backend/load_documents.py
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Reads all JSON files from insurance_rag_data/ and converts each entry into
a LangChain Document object for use in the RAG pipeline.

  Logic:
    - Iterates over every .json file in the DATA_DIR ("insurance_rag_data")
    - Opens and parses each file
    - For each item in the "documents" array, creates a Document with:
        page_content = "{title}. {content}"
        metadata = {
          id:          unique document ID (e.g. "CP_001")
          category:    document category (e.g. "Claim_Process")
          tags:        list of keyword tags
          importance:  "High", "Medium", or "Low"
          source_file: the filename it came from
        }
    - Returns the full list of Document objects

  Can be run standalone to verify loading:
    python load_documents.py
    Prints total document count and shows a sample document

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  backend/insurance_rag_data/ — Knowledge Base JSON Files
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Seven JSON files that form the domain knowledge base for the RAG chatbot.
All follow the same schema:

  {
    "domain": "Indian Health Insurance",
    "documents": [
      {
        "id":         "UNIQUE_ID",
        "category":   "Category_Name",
        "title":      "Short title",
        "content":    "Detailed explanation...",
        "tags":       ["tag1", "tag2"],
        "importance": "High | Medium | Low"
      }
    ]
  }

  claims_process.json (2 documents)
    Category: Claim_Process
    - CP_001 Cashless Claim: Hospital sends pre-auth to insurer/TPA.
      After approval, insurer directly settles the hospital bill.
    - CP_002 Reimbursement Claim: Insured pays hospital first, then submits
      bills, discharge summary, and prescriptions for settlement.

  disease_coverage.json (3 documents)
    Category: Disease_Coverage
    - DC_001 Diabetes: Chronic pre-existing disease. 24-month waiting period.
      Hospitalization and complications covered. OPD excluded.
    - DC_002 Heart Disease: 36-month waiting period. Angioplasty, bypass
      surgery, and ICU care covered after waiting period.
    - DC_003 Hypertension: 24-month waiting period. Stroke and cardiac
      complications covered after waiting period.

  glossary.json (2 documents)
    Category: Glossary
    - GL_001 TPA: Third Party Administrator — manages claims and cashless services
    - GL_002 Network Hospital: Hospitals tied with insurer for cashless treatment

  policy_concepts.json (3 documents)
    Category: Policy_Concept
    - PC_001 Sum Insured: Maximum amount payable by insurer in a policy year.
      Once exhausted, no further claims unless restoration benefit applies.
    - PC_002 Co-payment: Percentage of claim paid by insured. Common in senior
      citizen policies to control claim frequency and premium.
    - PC_003 Waiting Period: Initial time during which specific diseases are not
      covered. Pre-existing diseases have longer waiting periods.

  policy_types.json (2 documents)
    Category: Policy_Type
    - PT_001 Individual Policy: Separate sum insured for each insured person.
    - PT_002 Family Floater: Single sum insured shared by all family members.
      Cost-effective but risk of exhaustion if multiple claims occur.

  regulations_irdai.json (2 documents)
    Category: Regulation
    - IR_001 Portability: IRDAI rule allowing policyholders to switch insurers
      without losing waiting period credits if continuous coverage is maintained.
    - IR_002 Free Look Period: Policyholder can cancel policy within 15 days
      of receipt if terms are not satisfactory.

  risk_factors.json (2 documents)
    Category: Risk_Factor
    - RF_001 Age: Increasing age raises probability of hospitalization and
      chronic illness, leading to higher premiums.
    - RF_002 Smoking: Increases risk of cancer and heart disease, leading to
      higher claim frequency and premium loading.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  backend/insurance_faiss_db/
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pre-built FAISS vector index. Generated by running vector_store.py.
Loaded at server startup by main.py.

  index.faiss  — Binary FAISS index file containing dense vector embeddings
                 for all document chunks. Used for fast cosine similarity search.

  index.pkl    — Pickle file storing the document text and metadata associated
                 with each vector in the index. Required to retrieve the actual
                 content after a similarity search.

  Both files are listed in .gitignore and should be regenerated locally.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  backend/insurance_premium_model.pkl
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Serialised XGBoost regression model trained on indian_insurance_dataset.csv.
Loaded at startup via joblib.load(). Used by the /predict endpoint.

  Algorithm:    XGBoost Regressor (gradient boosting)
  Trees:        300 decision trees (num_trees: 300, num_parallel_tree: 1)
  Features:     7 input features (see encoding table below)
  Output:       Predicted annual premium in INR (integer)

  Feature encoding:
    age          int    Raw age in years (e.g. 30)
    sex          int    1 = Male, 0 = Female
    bmi          float  Body Mass Index (e.g. 24.5)
    children     int    Number of dependent children (0–10)
    smoker       int    1 = Smoker, 0 = Non-smoker
    disease      int    0 = Asthma, 1 = Diabetes, 2 = Heart Disease,
                        3 = Hypertension, 4 = Multiple Conditions, 5 = None
    policy_type  int    0 = Family Floater, 1 = Individual, 2 = Senior Citizen

  The model is listed in .gitignore (*.pkl) and should be retrained or
  obtained separately.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  backend/insurance_premium_model.json
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The same XGBoost model exported in JSON format. Contains the full model
structure including all 300 trees, split conditions, split indices, base
weights, loss changes, and sum hessians for each node. Used for inspection,
portability, or loading via the XGBoost native API if needed.

  Key metadata visible in the JSON:
    feature_names: ["age","sex","bmi","children","smoker","disease","policy_type"]
    feature_types: ["int","int","float","int","int","int","int"]
    num_trees:     300
    estimator_type: regressor

